https://assets.codepen.io/t-1/chriscorner.png" style=3D"width: 65px; border=
-radius: 4px;" /> Chris=E2=80=99 Corner=0D=0A                            </=
h2>=0D=0A                            <p class=3D"corner-intro">A collection=
 of web design and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris C=
oyier.</p>=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                  =
        <h3>HTML</h3>=0A<p>I=E2=80=99m with Den Odell here in <a href=3D"ht=
tps://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/IKn3812Xg2oz_33F-ZTrEw~~/AABEfhA~/50bksj5s=
NtsZ5pJfIC-KyW6XydpSO0SV4Fi4Y5JUtpovWnR7_EiQjuKwdZGoeBSImHdqUEtoa-mbw1yjL12=
S7PC1h0S60rPelj6viTDeC3Vchyas5hBgILZIFidKDlvDWe-rdeXdEaSsx759JQD7_fzlex4X9H=
AXpDJMZ-WBBk8~">HTML=E2=80=99s Best Kept Secret: The <code>&lt;output&gt;</=
code> Tag</a>. I have a real affinity for that tag, perhaps because of it=
=E2=80=99s lovely balance pairing with <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>. If you=
=E2=80=99ve got any sort of value you=E2=80=99re displaying that is derived=
 from inputs, it=E2=80=99s the perfect tag. Not just semantically, but acce=
ssibly too.</p>=0A<blockquote><p>Most have never touched it. Some don=E2=80=
=99t even know it exists.</p>=0A<p>That=E2=80=99s a shame, because it solve=
s something we=E2=80=99ve been cobbling together with <code>&lt;div></code>=
s and <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/zIuVCw5SgOOBRpOWdc5wpA~=
~/AABEfhA~/PIIbWd_AZp4oqduYGepafKq7ST0UtTjyaN49af0sz0dP1YTWkAEmxeaY5mtcdqC9=
LenWkypajXCP6ugtq1A-4RAqe58Bdlxf0gCh5c5QiE4DQTYafSBcXOyGVJdFZolJrbeMAr6S9mB=
0zBuRTg-FBt74Swzeo3vKuU4cVWqKC-M~">ARIA</a> for years: dynamic results that=
 are announced to screen readers by default.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>So imagi=
ne like two numbers inputs where you add the numbers together and show the =
result. <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/7SoGwrybTDl0RvJdgLEYP=
A~~/AABEfhA~/xwClsdhVMEJwfa84u1tOs-MOxYludBzzUia3F6Sc5jYpDrCTjMRqG8AZkPHhEw=
_WlrWWiWsgC-tHwIX_331EkRtAGBLU1n4b0vogHMlJq1i7POCMJR7ymwtHOxy5PfPDwGxswoEbB=
PncW4cnJFIl-2vJHLKa7f3xETY3gNtjZuPgyhJno9Gcy423AWINqWj9">Just an example</a=
> meant to get you thinking about any sort of input/output scenario:</p>=0A=
<img src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenassets.com/cdn-c=
gi/image/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/output_input_=
example.png" alt=3D"Two number input fields with an addition sign between t=
hem. The left input reads 9, the right reads 4. The output following the eq=
uals sign is 13. " width=3D"400"/>=0A<p>If you are working with specificall=
y with <code>&lt;input type=3D&quot;number&quot;&gt;</code>, Ollie Williams=
 has some nicely-explained and practical information in <a href=3D"https://=
post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/ogr7B76ZoAa1tG3_Ja5x0w~~/AABEfhA~/50bksj5sNtsZ5p=
JfIC-KyRcF0HSJoWofKnDN13J5VX-nbI7S6N7EefT7xJ-HB0_A5XQ2oDyxzSsGtDyKIrmeSWcyI=
gVl0loWMe4nudoSG8BUM28RzCVLLBERQzLFogQKrMtakB_zzDBU53culBa1n1vLRl2J9kdvyMeI=
UJLtSok~">A complete guide to the HTML number input</a>. I really dig Ollie=
=E2=80=99s binding of <em>custom commands</em> to the inputs.</p>=0A=0A<pre=
 style=3D"font-size: 12px"><code class=3D"html">&lt;button commandfor=3D&qu=
ot;cost&quot; command=3D&quot;--decrement&quot;&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;=0A&lt;i=
nput id=3D&quot;cost&quot; type=3D&quot;number&quot; min=3D&quot;0.01&quot;=
 max=3D&quot;10&quot; step=3D&quot;0.01&quot; /&gt;=0A&lt;button commandfor=
=3D&quot;cost&quot; command=3D&quot;--increment&quot;&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;=
=0A</code></pre>=0A<p>Those things that look like CSS Custom Properties are=
 custom identifiers that you can listen for in JavaScript and call you own =
function. They show up as <code>event.command</code>, and as Ollie shows:</=
p>=0A=0A<pre style=3D"font-size: 12px"><code class=3D"js">const numberInput=
s =3D document.querySelectorAll('input[type=3D&quot;number&quot;]');=0Anumb=
erInputs.forEach((input) =3D&gt; {=0A  input.addEventListener(&quot;command=
&quot;, (event) =3D&gt; {=0A=09if (event.command =3D=3D=3D &quot;--incremen=
t&quot;) {=0A=09=09input.stepUp();=0A=09} else if (event.command =3D=3D=3D =
&quot;--decrement&quot;) {=0A=09=09=09input.stepDown();=0A=09}=0A  });=0A})=
;=0A</code></pre>=0A=0A<p>I guess we=E2=80=99re doing HTML now!</p>=0A<p>Sp=
eaking of HTML-focused APIs, Christian Heilmann has a reminder about one, i=
f you=E2=80=99re like me, you didn=E2=80=99t even know was there despite be=
ing quite old. <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/u4Tm4y7PMc0lYD=
WYVR-PPQ~~/AABEfhA~/rEsj4Ay8kugakZy8ExcXedd8XfaAqTjEJ73ynJu7pytwYM7maBAUq_K=
8KwpHnyeQts_oaJLVbW7cuTF5EBATQL7JvsoFyomCgeUlD46qJnwEVcvGbTePHKdzEhRfm5tlpT=
WvVf04PKRKxKniw0yRtBJSlfFN4npt1B5ei33HHFly6_YDp1MzquKaTlyAqwmwtzLNOhXUdV7sr=
-YzRMplo5CIYJoG-d5qMo1-pLKgKnE~">Abandonware of the web: do you know that t=
here is an HTML tables API</a>=3F Essentially if you have a reference to a =
literal <code><table></code> element in HTML you get methods you can call o=
n it like <code>.insertRow()</code> (and <code>.insertCell()</code> if you=
=E2=80=99ve got a row). I mean, OK.</p>=0A<p>One of the great things about =
HTML, <em>maybe even</em> the very greatest, is that it=E2=80=99s resilient=
 to problems. Like, you can and should produce valid HTML, but a mistake or=
 presence of something the current browser doesn=E2=80=99t understand isn=
=E2=80=99t a show-stopper like it can be in other languages like JavaScript=
.</p>=0A<p><a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/6VVacp16qMHxckalL=
8_q_g~~/AABEfhA~/5c-fA7fpFv8UgwK2Om4rpeSd3ftkW4Q73ynXQgvr0q_eyix7MqMdfKI-n3=
iao6rVNUUHQaDhVjZkiIdQwxehlwhmXvSz7u1G7xcuyGGFOU0_GWFGTQt3QS0i4j8TYjvPMD_7e=
KRsn3wm5hjXz6xV7w~~">Patrick Brosset shows off</a> this gnarly =E2=80=9Ccom=
plete=E2=80=9D HTML document:</p>=0A=0A<pre style=3D"font-size: 12px"><code=
 class=3D"html">&lt;html&gt;=0A  &lt;body marginheight=3D150 marginwidth=3D=
300 bgcolor=3Dblack text=3Dwhite&gt;=0A    &lt;marquee&gt;=0A      &lt;b&gt=
;Hello &lt;i&gt;HTML&lt;/b&gt; World!&lt;/i&gt;=0A    &lt;/marquee&gt;=0A</=
code></pre>=0A=0A<p>A validator isn=E2=80=99t going to like it, but as Patr=
ick says: =E2=80=9Cthe resulting page just loads and works fine in browsers=
.=E2=80=9D <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/PcBnYnqSvHHJKkmzf=
UAnCQ~~/AABEfhA~/xwClsdhVMEJwfa84u1tOs-MOxYludBzzUia3F6Sc5jYpDrCTjMRqG8AZkP=
HhEw_WF1E-NOKLhJz3NmpadY9B2VVRcBex8himDQ-eV12V24VI9oTVFMayXXDMcyIc5ZUvlKkhC=
IpQA7ydjhFXJ9I3l1Bna3zh73vg0kqMNf0OZon_5Vv7MqUsva3ifloydXGH">Sure does</a>.=
</p>=0A=0A<p>Another element that probably doesn=E2=80=99t get used as much=
 as it should is the <code>&lt;time&gt;</code> element, which is semantical=
ly appropriate for almost any sort of date/time. But maybe like Nolan Lawso=
ns says: <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/Ud9vn5WXQdJUG5VlJmE8=
og~~/AABEfhA~/-9NwjVxfm1DFyYyKcnMZk2UHtZn1m3Oae78vllTA5AiOTLzsr-2Z12iMyQ2j6=
c3lGDdQk8n0QWcyAaDUDDpu0lox8dpvXALKHP6ReeO2exj_JtBHU3ZNZcsmFY1C5ywq0dbfimol=
cSgEboyQJZ09VvALfIZirVonCWHzqj6bfuSoL6RNqH9gvoA0gd1D75n7">The <code>&lt;tim=
e&gt;</code> element should actually do something</a>. I enjoy using it cor=
rectly, but it would be cool to incentivize it with something a little more=
 meaty. Fair is fair though, there are screen reader implications:</p>=0A<b=
lockquote><p><a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/k3jEQ-eeSB_dtIcn=
mHjJ5Q~~/AABEfhA~/1AaVHi3KpTwA4HQw3YyeLuIXNUrT9Hjc-zxAawPTmNuM10s4mwvwbxV8y=
zyChNA2ol1_82pENwDoTQ-KAITNgxKaMyP4JA9ASWeTvByHFg6fwb_6ZOhAyqmNxSZCLd9rVa4S=
05pExOoOEzUe3fpU9g~~">L=C3=A9onie Watson helpfully reports</a> <em>that the=
 screen readers NVDA and Narrator actually do read out the timestamp in a h=
uman-readable format! So <code>&lt;time></code> does have an impact on acce=
ssibility (although arguably not a positive one)</em>.</p></blockquote>=0A<=
p>I=E2=80=99ll end with mentioning that <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechn=
ol.com/f/a/V1atwrvFc7L8O4czd0BrbA~~/AABEfhA~/sedHnBPDddsuZLa2XJ0yFfwMjer9vO=
qRlowpgEHImd_o2uiB-lo9IQdn9rWAX9AylKVrRMaEJ19OQ1xdRiwIvUjXX7HeAqTNR2KYjtY4s=
NNXALPt9oOIv465a6POHNj7">HTML Day is Saturday, August 8th, 2026</a>.</p>=0D=
